<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Minas Gerais</h1><p class="st-meta">1720 CE – 1821 CE · capital Vila Rica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The gold and diamond region of colonial Brazil, whose wealth funded Portugal's 18th century and whose baroque towns are among the finest in the Americas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gold was discovered in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> in the 1690s, starting the largest gold rush in the colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. After a tax revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica</a> in 1720 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filipe-dos-santos">Filipe dos Santos</a>, the crown separated the mining region from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-paulo">Captaincy of São Paulo</a> as its own captaincy, with Vila Rica, now Ouro Preto, as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The crown taxed gold through the royal fifth and strictly controlled the region. Diamonds were found around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamantina">Tijuco</a>, now Diamantina, in the 1720s and became a royal monopoly. Hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans worked the mines. Wealthy brotherhoods built baroque churches decorated by the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleijadinho">Aleijadinho</a>. As gold declined, a conspiracy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira">Inconfidência Mineira</a>, plotted independence in 1789; its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiradentes">Tiradentes</a> was hanged in 1792.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the gold ran low, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas</a> turned to farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. In 1821 it became a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>, and later one of its most populous states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-minas-gerais.jpg" alt="Captaincy of Minas Gerais" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Plovisanta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_de_Minas_Gerais.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1720 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vila Rica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gomes Freire de Andrade</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Minas Gerais</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1770&amp;focus=captaincy-of-minas-gerais" aria-label="Captaincy of Minas Gerais on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3494 2910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of Minas Gerais lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3814 3166L3944 2917M3814 3166L3718 3265"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0M3718 3265h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3814 3166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3814 3166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1770&amp;focus=captaincy-of-minas-gerais">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
